# NAME: Cinnado B6 (T23ZN, OS02G10, TM18NA LTE)
# FRAG: soc-xburst1 core ssl
#
# SoC:     Ingenic T23ZN, XBurst1 MIPS32 R1, 64MB RAM (46M+18M split)
# Sensor:  OmniVision OS02G10, 1/2.9" 2MP, I2C 0x3c, reg_addr_size=0 (ZRT bank-switch)
# Cellular: TUGE Technologies TM18NA (UNISOC V8850 baseband), USB (T23 dwc2 host mode)
# MCU:     MindMotion MM32F0020B1T (Cortex-M0), I2C addr 0x10, PIR/battery/power/watchdog
# Flash:   16MB NOR SPI
#
# NOTES:
#  - This is the LTE sibling of cinnado_b6_t23zn_os02g10_atbm6461 (same
#    "Cinnado B6" chassis/PTZ family, T23ZN + OS02G10), but with a TM18NA LTE
#    module in place of the ATBM6461 WiFi/SDIO chip. Reverse-engineered from
#    stock firmware firmware_T23ZN_20260803_2026.bin -- no live hardware access
#    yet, so PTZ/GPIO map below is carried over from the WiFi sibling's own
#    "best available guess... verify on hardware" starting point, not confirmed
#    for this exact variant.
#  - CRITICAL: the stock firmware disables the MCU's hardware watchdog very
#    early in boot (before its normal init sequence) via a dedicated recovery
#    tool. See package/thingino-mcu-wdt, which ships that same extracted vendor
#    binary and runs it from an F00a-prefixed script -- before F00ledd, ahead
#    of the rest of rcF/rcS. If first boot reboot-loops, this is the first
#    thing to suspect (watchdog window tighter than an F-script can catch).
#  - MCU I2C bus CONFIRMED via live hardware + vendor kernel binary analysis:
#    it's GPIO-bitbanged (CONFIG_SOFT_I2C1_GPIO_V12_JZ), SDA=GPIO53/SCL=GPIO52
#    (GPIO_PB(21)/GPIO_PB(20)), not the I2C1_V12_JZ hardware controller. Wired
#    up via thingino-kopt's I2C1 "GPIO bitbang" mode (package/thingino-kopt),
#    which drives both the Kconfig fixups and the Pike/board.h +
#    gpio_customized.c pin/pull-up generation at build time -- see
#    scripts/patch_kernel_i2c1_gpio_pins.sh and
#    scripts/patch_kernel_i2c1_gpio_pullups.sh.
#  - USB uses thingino-kopt's DWC2_WIFI_ONLY option (properly, via kopt, not
#    a raw fragment). An earlier attempt at this pinned the "Starting
#    kernel..." zero-output hang on this option specifically, but that test
#    was confounded: the pull-up generation script had a bug (fixed below)
#    that enabled Pike/gpio_customized.c's arch_initcall unconditionally,
#    regardless of the pull-up Kconfig setting -- so every "DWC2_WIFI_ONLY"
#    test up to that point still had the real crash cause (arch_initcall)
#    silently active too. Once genuinely isolated, arch_initcall alone was
#    enough to crash even with the known-good minimal USB config, and
#    DWC2_WIFI_ONLY was never actually tested cleanly on its own. Re-enabled
#    here for a proper isolated test now that the confound is gone.
#  - BR2_THINGINO_I2C1_GPIO_PULLUP: an earlier attempt at this (via
#    arch_initcall) reproduced a zero-output boot hang, isolated on live
#    hardware with the USB config otherwise matching the known-good
#    baseline. Likely cause: arch_initcall runs too early for
#    jz_gpio_set_func() to operate safely. Fixed using late_initcall instead
#    (see scripts/patch_kernel_i2c1_gpio_pullups.sh) -- confirmed live: pulls
#    apply cleanly at boot (dmesg shows "set gpio pull: 52-90"/"53-90"), no
#    hang. Kept enabled since it's a real, verified improvement (matches
#    stock firmware's own config) even though it turned out NOT to be what
#    was causing the separate watchdog-disable failure below (ruled out:
#    same failure with pull-ups on or off) -- that was a bug in one specific
#    vendor binary, not a bus/timing issue. See the mcu_test note below.
#  - PTZ motors DISABLED (BR2_THINGINO_MOTORS) for now: the WiFi sibling's
#    copied gpio_tilt map claimed GPIO52/53 for motor ST2/ST3, which directly
#    conflicts with the real MCU I2C pins above -- proof the whole PTZ GPIO
#    map carried over from the sibling is unreliable for this variant, not
#    just those two pins. Needs its own hardware-verification pass before
#    re-enabling. Confirmed via live hardware that a STALE built rootfs (built
#    once early in this board's bring-up before motors were disabled here)
#    kept shipping the motor package's files even after this option was
#    turned off -- buildroot doesn't prune a deselected package's already-
#    installed target files. Always `make clean` before `make all` for this
#    board, not a targeted/incremental rebuild.
#  - MCU responds reliably at I2C address 0x10, NOT 0x11. package/thingino-
#    mcu-wdt ships the vendor's mcu_test diagnostic tool (+ its libcommon.so
#    dependency), which already uses the correct address/bus, and runs
#    `mcu_test --wdt_disable` -- confirmed live: succeeds cleanly (exit 0,
#    no errors) every time. An earlier version of this package instead
#    shipped a different vendor binary, disable_mcu_wdt (from a separate
#    recovery-initramfs tool, hardcoded to address 0x11 and /dev/i2c-2,
#    binary-patched to 0x10/i2c-1) -- even after that patch, it reliably
#    failed with garbled I2C responses. Ruled out as a bus/timing/pull-up
#    issue (mcu_test succeeds on the identical bus, same session, same
#    pull-up config); the bug is specific to that one binary's own protocol
#    handling. Use mcu_test, not disable_mcu_wdt -- don't re-introduce it.
#  - LTE modem (TM18NA/Unisoc, USB VID:PID 1782:4d13) enumerates via the
#    kernel's built-in "option" USB-serial driver -- CONFIG_USB_SERIAL_OPTION
#    comes from BR2_PACKAGE_USBNET_WWAN_SERIAL below, already selected.
#    Confirmed live on stock firmware: the modem enumerates twice (bare
#    AT-only, then re-enumerates as composite: cdc_ether/eth0 + 5 AT/diag
#    ttyUSB ports 0-4) with NO host-side trigger needed -- this is the
#    module's own autonomous behavior, not something unisoc_mgr's proprietary
#    stack does, so it should occur identically under this Thingino kernel.
#    Stock firmware's own app never actually uses eth0/DHCP for data (0
#    RX/TX packets observed over 80+ seconds of live uptime) -- it drives an
#    embedded AT+CIPSTART/AT+CIPRXGET TCP-over-AT socket API instead, which
#    Thingino has no equivalent of. For Thingino, the standard USB_SERIAL_
#    WWAN/pppd dial-up path (already selected below) is the practical way to
#    get real IP connectivity: dial ATD*99# after setting the APN via
#    AT+CGDCONT on the modem's AT-command port. CONFIRMED live on stock
#    firmware: /dev/ttyUSB0 is the real AT-command port (responds "OK" to a
#    plain "AT" probe; it's also the same port unisoc_mgr itself continuously
#    polls with AT^TGTVSIMSTATUS?). ttyUSB1-4 gave no response to "AT" --
#    likely diagnostic/GPS-only interfaces, not usable for dialing. The ppp
#    peer file added this session (package/usbnet/files/wwan-peer) already
#    targets ttyUSB0 on this basis. Still needs: a real carrier APN in
#    /etc/ppp/scripts/wwan-connect (placeholder "internet" won't work) before
#    `pppd call wwan` will actually get online.
#  - USB MODEM STILL DOESN'T ENUMERATE under this Thingino build (`lsusb`
#    shows only the root hub) -- confirmed NOT an MCU/I2C issue: the
#    watchdog-disable command now succeeds cleanly (see mcu_test above) and
#    `master_power_on` fires identically to stock on every boot, so modem
#    power isn't gated behind any single MCU command we've found. Ruled out
#    this session via kernel source tracing (not just guessing): VBUS
#    control in drivers/usb/dwc2/dwc2_jz.c's dwc2_vbus_work() is a
#    confirmed no-op on this board (both the regulator and the drvvbus GPIO
#    are absent -- zero "vbus" strings appear anywhere in dmesg, matching
#    the code's own early-return path exactly), so it's not a VBUS-enable
#    problem either. Live lead not yet followed up: every boot logs
#    `dwc2 dwc2: ID PIN CHANGED!` (drivers/usb/dwc2/core.c,
#    dwc2_handle_conn_id_status_change_intr) shortly after USB init, which
#    schedules otg_id_work -- but none of that work's own follow-up log
#    lines (e.g. "init DWC as A_HOST", or the "Connection id status change
#    timed out" warning) ever appear in dmesg, meaning it's unclear whether
#    that work actually completes. Worth checking directly next: does
#    otg_id_work run to completion, and does dwc2_is_host_mode() ever
#    return true on this board's ID-pin wiring. A live D+/D- logic-analyzer
#    capture (already used earlier in this project to compare stock vs.
#    Thingino) comparing against a stock-firmware capture may be the more
#    direct next step if source tracing doesn't resolve it.
#  - BR2_THINGINO_DWC2_PROBE_DELAY_MS=3000: a real, confirmed timing
#    difference vs stock was found: stock loads dwc2 as a loadable module
#    (`insmod /lib/modules/dwc2.ko` from rcS, several seconds into boot,
#    after rootfs mount/hostname/telnetd), while our kernel fixups build
#    it INTO the kernel (CONFIG_USB_JZ_DWC2=y), probing at ~0.15s --
#    during early kernel boot, before userspace exists. Theory: the
#    modem's own baseband/PHY power-up takes longer than that, so
#    Thingino's controller may finish its initial attach-detection before
#    the modem is even electrically present. This new kernel option
#    (package/all-patches/linux/3.10.14/0092-dwc2-jz-optional-probe-delay.patch,
#    wired via thingino-kopt) adds an opt-in msleep() at the very start of
#    dwc2_jz_probe(), defaulting to 0/no-op for every other board. 3000ms
#    chosen to comfortably exceed stock's own rcS timing. Live-tested a
#    related but NOT equivalent experiment this session (unbind/rebind
#    the already-probed jz-dwc2 platform driver at 600+ seconds uptime,
#    well after the modem should be ready) -- that specific test was
#    inconclusive (the rebind itself failed with -ENOMEM, a real but
#    separate driver limitation around repeated bind/unbind cycles not
#    cleanly releasing DMA/IRQ resources, not a signal about the modem).
#    A clean first-boot-with-delay test has NOT been done yet -- this is
#    the next thing to verify on the next hardware flash.
BR2_INGENIC_SOC_MODEL="t23zn"
BR2_ISP_CLKA_600MHZ=y
BR2_ISP_CLKA_SCLKA=y
BR2_ISP_CLK_200MHZ=y
BR2_ISP_CLK_SCLKA=y
BR2_ISP_PRINT_LEVEL_0=y
BR2_PACKAGE_BUSYBOX_SHOW_OTHERS=y
BR2_PACKAGE_I2C_TOOLS=y
BR2_PACKAGE_THINGINO_KOPT_DWC2=y
BR2_PACKAGE_THINGINO_KOPT_DWC2_WIFI_ONLY=y
BR2_PACKAGE_THINGINO_KOPT_I2C=y
BR2_PACKAGE_THINGINO_KOPT_I2C_BUS1=y
BR2_PACKAGE_THINGINO_KOPT_I2C_BUS1_GPIO=y
BR2_PACKAGE_THINGINO_KOPT_MMC0=y
BR2_PACKAGE_THINGINO_KOPT_MMC0_PB_4BIT=y
BR2_PACKAGE_THINGINO_MCU_WDT=y
BR2_PACKAGE_USBNET=y
BR2_PACKAGE_USBNET_WWAN_SERIAL=y
BR2_SENSOR_1_NAME="os02g10"
BR2_THINGINO_AUDIO=y
BR2_THINGINO_AUDIO_GPIO=63
BR2_THINGINO_DWC2_PROBE_DELAY_MS=3000
BR2_THINGINO_I2C1_GPIO_PULLUP=y
BR2_THINGINO_I2C1_GPIO_SCL=52
BR2_THINGINO_I2C1_GPIO_SDA=53
BR2_THINGINO_RMEM_MB=18
BR2_THINGINO_SDCARD=y
FLASH_SIZE_MB=16
